        "tipo": "gramatica",
        "min": 10,
        "titulo": "Colors, and the adjective after «sein»",
        "explicacao": "STEP 1: to describe something with «sein», the adjective comes AFTER the verb and does NOT change: «Die Jacke ist blau», «Die Schuhe sind schwarz» — exactly like English \"the jacket is blue\", \"the shoes are black\". STEP 2: ask «Welche Farbe hat …?» (what color is …?) or «Wie ist …?». STEP 3: colors are written in lowercase, because they're adjectives. STEP 4: the adjective can take «zu» (too): «Die Hose ist zu groß» (the pants are too big). Adjective endings in front of a noun (ein blaues Hemd) only come at A2; for now, use the adjective ON ITS OWN, after «sein».",
        "comparacao": "Good news: after «sein», German adjectives behave exactly like English ones — no ending at all: «ist blau», «sind blau», \"is blue\", \"are blue\". Several colors are almost English: braun (brown), grün (green), grau (gray), weiß (white).",
